WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:03.570
-How are you, Colin?
-Whoo! Hi, Seth. How are you?

00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:07.206
-I miss having your energy
in the studio with me.

00:00:07.206 --> 00:00:09.208
-Thank you. I miss being there.

00:00:09.208 --> 00:00:11.110
This is gonna be weird,
but you like this

00:00:11.110 --> 00:00:14.713
long, Hitchcockian shot
behind me, right?

00:00:14.713 --> 00:00:17.617
-The cinematographer in you
has come out once again

00:00:17.617 --> 00:00:20.253
with your framing there.

00:00:20.253 --> 00:00:21.654
I think the whole interview

00:00:21.654 --> 00:00:24.056
I'll be waiting to see
who's coming up behind you.

00:00:24.056 --> 00:00:25.758
-Well, I think
it's also symbolic,

00:00:25.758 --> 00:00:27.693
like the unconscious and --
you know what I mean?

00:00:27.693 --> 00:00:30.029
-I think we all get it. We all
get what you're trying to do.

00:00:30.029 --> 00:00:31.698
-Oh, I don't know about that,
but go ahead.

00:00:31.698 --> 00:00:33.299
-[ Laughs ]

00:00:33.299 --> 00:00:36.168
You are obviously
a born and raised New Yorker,

00:00:36.168 --> 00:00:37.569
and I need to know.

00:00:37.569 --> 00:00:40.640
Did you leave -- Have you left
at all during quarantine?

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:43.176
-Just little minor trips,
but not really, no.

00:00:43.176 --> 00:00:45.712
I'm one of those --
I'm like those people, you know,

00:00:45.712 --> 00:00:48.915
that don't leave during
the hurricane and the tornado.

00:00:48.915 --> 00:00:51.016
The old guy that's like,
"I'm not leaving my house."

00:00:51.016 --> 00:00:52.252
And then they die,
and you're like,

00:00:52.252 --> 00:00:53.753
"You didn't want to leave."

00:00:53.753 --> 00:00:56.089
-It does seem like this is --

00:00:56.089 --> 00:00:57.990
It's like the source
of your powers.

00:00:57.990 --> 00:01:01.227
It would seem very strange to me
if you left New York City.

00:01:01.227 --> 00:01:03.162
-You're right.
Every time I leave,

00:01:03.162 --> 00:01:05.097
when I come back, I almost --

00:01:05.097 --> 00:01:08.368
my whole heart swells up in
the cab coming from the airport,

00:01:08.368 --> 00:01:11.537
like, "Oh-ohh." I love New York.

00:01:11.537 --> 00:01:13.472
-It's a beautiful drive
from the airport.

00:01:13.472 --> 00:01:15.241
It should be noted, for those
who have never been here.

00:01:15.241 --> 00:01:17.477
-Really is. Yeah.

00:01:17.477 --> 00:01:20.746
-So, this is based on your --
the book is based on your show

00:01:20.746 --> 00:01:22.215
"Red State, Blue State."

00:01:22.215 --> 00:01:25.317
And you're not that optimistic
about the state of the nation.

00:01:25.317 --> 00:01:27.220
You basically say we're like --

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:31.224
it's like a marriage couple
about to sign divorce papers.

00:01:31.224 --> 00:01:34.393
-Yeah, I mean, obviously,
now that everybody --

00:01:34.393 --> 00:01:36.228
everybody knows this is over.

00:01:36.228 --> 00:01:38.430
But we still keep hanging on
like any marriage.

00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:41.000
You're like, "They'll come
around. They'll change."

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:43.168
You know?
And what are we arguing over?

00:01:43.168 --> 00:01:45.437
Money, like any married couple.

00:01:45.437 --> 00:01:47.640
But the good thing
about my book, if I --

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:51.177
if I can say so,
is everything's a state.

00:01:51.177 --> 00:01:53.813
So it's like -- I'm all about
table of contents at this point.

00:01:53.813 --> 00:01:55.981
-[ Laughs ]
-You can read this book

00:01:55.981 --> 00:01:57.750
and just there it is.

00:01:57.750 --> 00:02:00.319
It's like -- You ever see "The
Founder" with Michael Keaton?

00:02:00.319 --> 00:02:01.854
-Yeah, of course.

00:02:01.854 --> 00:02:05.124
-And then he walks in McDonald's
and everything's organized.

00:02:05.124 --> 00:02:07.427
That's how people are gonna feel
when they look at this book.

00:02:07.427 --> 00:02:09.362
They go, "Oh, look, New Mexico."

00:02:09.362 --> 00:02:11.431
Instead of like,
"Oh, French fries."

00:02:11.431 --> 00:02:12.765
You know, like that.
-[ Laughs ]

00:02:12.765 --> 00:02:15.634
So people will respond
to your book the way

00:02:15.634 --> 00:02:18.171
Ray Kroc did when he saw
the original McDonald's?

00:02:18.171 --> 00:02:20.005
-Exactly.

00:02:20.005 --> 00:02:22.975
-The dedication -- I'm always
drawn to the dedication.

00:02:22.975 --> 00:02:25.445
You dedicated this book
to someone very special.

00:02:25.445 --> 00:02:26.578
-Yeah.

00:02:26.578 --> 00:02:28.514
I dedicated it to myself.
-Right.

00:02:28.514 --> 00:02:31.817
-Because one thing I'm getting
sick of is the false humility.

00:02:31.817 --> 00:02:34.787
You know, that was,
like, a 1950s thing.

00:02:34.787 --> 00:02:37.055
And then everybody
made fun of show business

00:02:37.055 --> 00:02:38.591
in the '70s and '60s.

00:02:38.591 --> 00:02:41.928
And Dustin Hoffman would
wear, like, a corduroy jacket

00:02:41.928 --> 00:02:45.264
to the Oscars, and you know,
now, suddenly it's back to

00:02:45.264 --> 00:02:47.399
"You're amazing.
You're the best,"

00:02:47.399 --> 00:02:50.370
and people are hugging and
over-complimenting each other

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:52.739
and dedicating,
making these platitudes.

00:02:52.739 --> 00:02:54.840
So I dedicated
to the person that did

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:57.210
the lion's share of the work
in this country -- me.

00:02:57.210 --> 00:03:00.647
-It is. It's very refreshing
for someone to just call it --

00:03:00.647 --> 00:03:04.049
Look, I think we all know
you did the lion's share

00:03:04.049 --> 00:03:06.285
of the work here,
and you deserve the dedication.

00:03:06.285 --> 00:03:07.720
-I mean, it was a Trump move.

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:09.722
I guess this is the right era
to do that, too.

00:03:09.722 --> 00:03:11.624
-[ Laughs ]

00:03:11.624 --> 00:03:14.827
We have a debate coming up
tomorrow night.

00:03:14.827 --> 00:03:17.129
I think a lot of people
would love to see Colin Quinn

00:03:17.129 --> 00:03:18.864
as a moderator
for a presidential debate.

00:03:18.864 --> 00:03:20.700
What would be a question
you would ask?

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:22.468
-I would -- Well, one question
I would ask.

00:03:22.468 --> 00:03:24.970
I would say, "Are you as
frightened as everybody else

00:03:24.970 --> 00:03:26.606
that one of you actually will be
the president in November?"

00:03:26.606 --> 00:03:29.309
-[ Laughs ]

00:03:29.309 --> 00:03:30.609
Yeah.

00:03:30.609 --> 00:03:32.712
It does feel very surreal
that it's finally happening.

00:03:32.712 --> 00:03:35.014
Like, I knew it was coming,
and now, I kind of can't

00:03:35.014 --> 00:03:36.649
wrap my head around the fact
that we're gonna watch

00:03:36.649 --> 00:03:40.119
those two gentlemen
talk tomorrow.

00:03:40.119 --> 00:03:42.221
-Kind of exciting, in a way,
you know what I mean?

00:03:42.221 --> 00:03:44.757
-[ Laughs ]

00:03:44.757 --> 00:03:48.061
You take issue with some of
the things that I feel like

00:03:48.061 --> 00:03:51.931
are obviously held in
very high regard,

00:03:51.931 --> 00:03:53.065
like the Bill of Rights.

00:03:53.065 --> 00:03:54.834
You have some issues
with the Bill of Rights.

00:03:54.834 --> 00:03:56.869
-Well, I mean,
the Bill of Rights is the worst.

00:03:56.869 --> 00:03:59.305
It was an add-on anyway. It's
not part of the Constitution.

00:03:59.305 --> 00:04:01.340
They were like, "Hey, we forgot
about the people."

00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:02.441
And then they were like,
"Oh, let's give them

00:04:02.441 --> 00:04:03.475
the Bill of Rights."

00:04:03.475 --> 00:04:05.377
And first of all,
freedom of speech --

00:04:05.377 --> 00:04:06.679
that's a figure of speech.

00:04:06.679 --> 00:04:08.113
It's not supposed to be
freedom of speech.

00:04:08.113 --> 00:04:10.649
Now we've seen --
Social media has exposed

00:04:10.649 --> 00:04:12.351
how horrifying it is.

00:04:12.351 --> 00:04:15.855
Because our country is not meant
to be a political --

00:04:15.855 --> 00:04:18.824
We're not supposed to be
an intelligent country.

00:04:18.824 --> 00:04:22.561
We were like an enthusiastic,
like, young, like, jock.

00:04:22.561 --> 00:04:24.263
Fun, you know?

00:04:24.263 --> 00:04:25.764
We were supposed
to be like France,

00:04:25.764 --> 00:04:29.335
debating with chain smoking and,
you know, sitting in a café,

00:04:29.335 --> 00:04:31.270
talking about
the nuances of politics.

00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:32.971
And now that's
what we've become.

00:04:32.971 --> 00:04:36.442
-And I think one of the things
that, you know,

00:04:36.442 --> 00:04:38.344
whenever this comes up and there
is a seat on the Supreme Court,

00:04:38.344 --> 00:04:39.878
we realize that
we're a lot of people

00:04:39.878 --> 00:04:43.215
but not really a lot of us
have that much of a say.

00:04:43.215 --> 00:04:47.319
-Yeah, I mean, all these people,
all these senators, Congress

00:04:47.319 --> 00:04:49.188
who take care of everything --
Supreme Court.

00:04:49.188 --> 00:04:50.956
They're like,
"Yeah, leave it at nine."

00:04:50.956 --> 00:04:53.459
It's like, "Really? Isn't that
how the country started?

00:04:53.459 --> 00:04:54.593
With nine?"

00:04:54.593 --> 00:04:57.263
Wouldn't it be like 27 times
more people?

00:04:57.263 --> 00:04:58.197
27 times.

00:04:58.197 --> 00:05:00.699
It should be like 270 people

00:05:00.699 --> 00:05:02.034
since there's
300 million people.

00:05:02.034 --> 00:05:03.602
There were 1 million people
at the time.

00:05:03.602 --> 00:05:06.038
But they're like, "No, nine
people, who, you know, would

00:05:06.038 --> 00:05:08.674
rather be showing each other
pictures of their grandchildren.

00:05:08.674 --> 00:05:12.078
Let's make that the deciding
factor on everything."

00:05:12.078 --> 00:05:14.513
-Declaration of Independence.

00:05:14.513 --> 00:05:16.349
Surely you don't have an issue
with that.

00:05:16.349 --> 00:05:18.917
-Well, because,
you know, obviously,

00:05:18.917 --> 00:05:19.918
all men are created equal.

00:05:19.918 --> 00:05:21.654
If we set expectations --

00:05:21.654 --> 00:05:24.190
Instead, if they just said,
"All men are created equal.

00:05:24.190 --> 00:05:26.992
We know what you're gonna say."
-[ Laughs ]

00:05:26.992 --> 00:05:28.061
-And then people
would have been like,

00:05:28.061 --> 00:05:30.830
"Okay, as long as you know that,
go on."

00:05:30.830 --> 00:05:32.965
-And the electoral college.

00:05:32.965 --> 00:05:34.733
I think that people,
now more than ever,

00:05:34.733 --> 00:05:37.370
are maybe thinking this wasn't
the best situation?

00:05:37.370 --> 00:05:40.439
-Yeah, but at the time -- at
the time -- you look at a show

00:05:40.439 --> 00:05:43.308
like "Deadwood," which is where
I get all my research from.

00:05:43.308 --> 00:05:48.014
"Deadwood" -- you're dealing
with, like, psychotic guys,

00:05:48.014 --> 00:05:50.349
oil barons, cattle barons --

00:05:50.349 --> 00:05:52.685
they didn't want to be states.

00:05:52.685 --> 00:05:55.254
They needed incentive
to be a state.

00:05:55.254 --> 00:05:57.089
They're like, "Well, I'd rather
be a territory."

00:05:57.089 --> 00:06:00.826
Being a territory means you
still can do whatever you want,

00:06:00.826 --> 00:06:02.928
but you don't have to pay taxes,
so to get them in,

00:06:02.928 --> 00:06:04.130
they kind of had to
give them something,

00:06:04.130 --> 00:06:06.732
so they gave them two senators,
you know.

00:06:06.732 --> 00:06:09.501
-I also -- I believe
I understand that

00:06:09.501 --> 00:06:10.836
more from "Deadwood"
than anything else,

00:06:10.836 --> 00:06:12.904
so all jokes aside...

00:06:12.904 --> 00:06:14.741
-"Deadwood" says a lot,
you know?

00:06:14.741 --> 00:06:16.375
-Although the pulling
of the strings

00:06:16.375 --> 00:06:18.444
behind the scenes in Yankton...

00:06:18.444 --> 00:06:20.513
[ Laughter ]

00:06:20.513 --> 00:06:24.917
Hey, as a stand-up comedian,
I have to ask.

00:06:24.917 --> 00:06:26.285
How is this era?

00:06:26.285 --> 00:06:28.053
I mean, it's obviously
incredibly difficult for people

00:06:28.053 --> 00:06:30.022
who do that full time.

00:06:30.022 --> 00:06:33.091
And what do you think the future
of stand-up's gonna be?

00:06:33.091 --> 00:06:36.228
-I think the future of stand-up
is gonna be like --

00:06:36.228 --> 00:06:38.731
and I have been accused of using
this reference too often --

00:06:38.731 --> 00:06:41.066
"Deadwood." No.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:41.066 --> 00:06:43.236
-I think the future of
stand-up comedy --

00:06:43.236 --> 00:06:44.937
It could be like "Deadwood."

00:06:44.937 --> 00:06:46.472
Those outdoor shows, you know?

00:06:46.472 --> 00:06:49.808
I think the future of stand-up
is gonna be below the equator

00:06:49.808 --> 00:06:52.378
because only outdoor shows
are gonna matter.

00:06:52.378 --> 00:06:55.915
There's no indoor.
Indoor is done for society now.

00:06:55.915 --> 00:06:57.216
I thought it was all indoor.

00:06:57.216 --> 00:06:58.918
It's gonna be all outdoor,
you know?

00:06:58.918 --> 00:07:02.221
And so I think the big --
like baseball,

00:07:02.221 --> 00:07:04.756
the Dominicans became
the best baseball players

00:07:04.756 --> 00:07:06.759
because Dominican Republic
can play year-round.

00:07:06.759 --> 00:07:10.395
So anybody that lives down South
is gonna have a lot more sets.

00:07:10.395 --> 00:07:12.364
And that's gonna be
the big comedy boom.

00:07:12.364 --> 00:07:14.367
-Oh, so, basically,
the Northeast,

00:07:14.367 --> 00:07:16.435
which -- let's be honest --
has been a pipeline

00:07:16.435 --> 00:07:18.204
for a long time
as far as American comedy,

00:07:18.204 --> 00:07:19.805
you think it's gonna dry up?

00:07:19.805 --> 00:07:21.606
-Yeah, unless global warming
happens.

00:07:21.606 --> 00:07:23.008
You know what I mean?

00:07:23.008 --> 00:07:25.644
-And I know -- like, I remember
in my high school,

00:07:25.644 --> 00:07:28.014
there was a couple kids
who were good at baseball,

00:07:28.014 --> 00:07:30.015
and their parents sent them down
to Florida to live full time.

00:07:30.015 --> 00:07:31.717
Do you think that'll happen
where, like, a comedian

00:07:31.717 --> 00:07:33.852
has a couple good sets
in New York and they're like,

00:07:33.852 --> 00:07:35.321
"We got to get you down South"?

00:07:35.321 --> 00:07:36.889
-Yeah, for a really funny guy.

00:07:36.889 --> 00:07:39.425
They're gonna be like,
"We got to get you to Paraguay"

00:07:39.425 --> 00:07:42.528
or something like that.
-[ Laughs ]

00:07:42.528 --> 00:07:44.897
Thank you. It's always so great
to see you, Colin.

00:07:44.897 --> 00:07:47.066
Thanks for being here.
-Thanks for having me on.

